1. Weak service permissions / privilege escalation
ATSB found a LocalSystem service whose configuration could be modified by Authenticated Users, resulting in SYSTEM compromise.     ATSB SOE Penetration Test Repor…
Start as the standard user:
whoami

Get-CimInstance Win32_Service |
Select-Object Name,StartName,State,PathName |
Sort-Object Name

Then enumerate service security descriptors:
sc.exe query state= all

For anything interesting:
sc.exe sdshow SERVICE_NAME
sc.exe qc SERVICE_NAME

We specifically care about service ACL rights such as:
SERVICE_CHANGE_CONFIG
SERVICE_START
SERVICE_STOP
WRITE_DAC
WRITE_OWNER
GENERIC_WRITE
GENERIC_ALL

being granted to Users, Authenticated Users, Everyone or the current standard user.
Also check executable/path ACLs:
Get-CimInstance Win32_Service | ForEach-Object {
    [pscustomobject]@{
        Name = $_.Name
        Account = $_.StartName
        Path = $_.PathName
    }
}

Then:
icacls "C:\path\to\service.exe"
icacls "C:\path\to\service\directory"

Finding: standard user can modify configuration or replace/write a binary used by a SYSTEM service.
Evidence: whoami, sc qc, sc sdshow, and icacls.
2. Outbound C2-style communication
The ATSB Critical finding involved an unrestricted PowerShell/code-execution chain followed by an outbound callback.     ATSB SOE Penetration Test Repor…
We do not need a reverse shell to test this. Use a controlled benign endpoint.
Check DNS and TCP:
Resolve-DnsName example.com

Test-NetConnection example.com -Port 443
Test-NetConnection example.com -Port 80

If you have an Ionize-controlled HTTPS endpoint, even better:
Invoke-WebRequest "https://YOUR-CONTROLLED-ENDPOINT/" -UseBasicParsing

Also:
curl.exe -I https://YOUR-CONTROLLED-ENDPOINT/

If you control a non-standard listener:
Test-NetConnection YOUR-HOST -Port 8443
Test-NetConnection YOUR-HOST -Port 4443

Interesting: unrestricted arbitrary outbound connectivity from user processes, particularly uncommon ports.
Not automatically a finding: normal web access to 443.
Evidence: command + successful TCP connection / HTTP response.
3. Permissive application control / LOLBins
This is already one of our strongest parallels. ATSB specifically identified MSBuild, InstallUtil and MSHTA as application-control bypass opportunities.     ATSB SOE Penetration Test Repor…
We already have MSBuild = successful supplied-code execution.
Now enumerate relevant binaries:
$bins = @(
"C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework\v4.0.30319\MSBuild.exe",
"C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework64\v4.0.30319\MSBuild.exe",
"C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework\v4.0.30319\InstallUtil.exe",
"C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework64\v4.0.30319\InstallUtil.exe",
"C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework\v4.0.30319\csc.exe",
"C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework64\v4.0.30319\csc.exe",
"C:\Windows\System32\mshta.exe",
"C:\Windows\SysWOW64\mshta.exe"
)

$bins | ForEach-Object {
    [pscustomobject]@{
        Path = $_
        Exists = Test-Path $_
    }
}

For each present binary, first simply test whether Airlock prevents it launching:
"C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework64\v4.0.30319\InstallUtil.exe" /?

"C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework64\v4.0.30319\csc.exe" /?

C:\Windows\System32\mshta.exe

For MSBuild, retain the benign Console.WriteLine("Ionize test...") proof we already ran.
Finding: a standard user can use an approved/trusted binary to execute otherwise prohibited user-supplied code.
ATSB's recommendation was explicitly to restrict standard-user access to code-execution tools and unapproved executables/scripts/DLLs.     ATSB SOE Penetration Test Repor…
4. PowerShell CLM / FullLanguage
ATSB had CLM enabled but bypassable.     ATSB SOE Penetration Test Repor…
For us:
whoami
$ExecutionContext.SessionState.LanguageMode

We already got:
FullLanguage

Also:
powershell.exe -NoProfile -Command '$ExecutionContext.SessionState.LanguageMode'

and, if available:
pwsh.exe -NoProfile -Command '$ExecutionContext.SessionState.LanguageMode'

ASIC result: we don't need to demonstrate a CLM bypass because the standard user already starts in FullLanguage.
Capture that as evidence.
5. BGInfo code-execution exposure
ATSB used BGInfo as another application-control bypass.     ATSB SOE Penetration Test Repor…
Find it:
Get-ChildItem C:\ -Filter "Bginfo*.exe" -Recurse -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
Select-Object FullName

Also:
where.exe /r C:\ Bginfo.exe
where.exe /r C:\ Bginfo64.exe

And look at common enterprise locations:
Get-ChildItem "\\$env:USERDNSDOMAIN\NETLOGON" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue

If found:
Get-AuthenticodeSignature "C:\path\Bginfo64.exe" |
Format-List Status,SignerCertificate,Path

Then test whether the standard user can launch it:
"C:\path\Bginfo64.exe"

At this stage I would not try to reproduce the old VBS payload** unless we need proof after confirming BGInfo is permitted**.
Finding candidate: trusted BGInfo binary available to ordinary users and permitted through application control.
