7. DNS exfiltration capability
ATSB demonstrated arbitrary external DNS resolution.     ATSB SOE Penetration Test Repor…
Basic check:
Resolve-DnsName example.com
Resolve-DnsName www.google.com

Then use an Ionize-controlled DNS domain if you have one:
Resolve-DnsName "ionize-test-20261012.YOURDOMAIN"

or:
nslookup ionize-test-20261012.YOURDOMAIN

Check the authoritative DNS logs on your controlled side.
This proves arbitrary user-controlled labels can leave via DNS without transferring sensitive information.
Finding: depends heavily on expected DNS architecture. External recursive resolution alone isn't necessarily a vulnerability.
8. PowerShell Execution Policy
ATSB found users could change their own execution policy.     ATSB SOE Penetration Test Repor…
First:
Get-ExecutionPolicy -List

Then test CurrentUser modification:
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned

Run:
Get-ExecutionPolicy -List

Then restore the original value.
If the original CurrentUser state was Undefined:
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser Undefined

Also test Process scope:
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process Bypass
Get-ExecutionPolicy -List

Process scope disappearing when PowerShell closes is expected.
Important: Execution Policy is not itself a strong security boundary, so we'd assess this in combination with application control rather than automatically report it as Medium.
9. USB infiltration/exfiltration
ATSB could read and write an unapproved USB device.     ATSB SOE Penetration Test Repor…
Insert an ordinary USB.
Check detection:
Get-Disk
Get-Volume

Then:
Get-PnpDevice -PresentOnly |
Where-Object {$_.Class -match 'DiskDrive|USB'} |
Format-Table Class,FriendlyName,Status

Test copying only benign data both directions:
"Ionize USB Test" |
Set-Content "$env:USERPROFILE\Desktop\Ionize-USB-Test.txt"

Then:
Copy-Item "$env:USERPROFILE\Desktop\Ionize-USB-Test.txt" E:\
Copy-Item E:\Ionize-USB-Test.txt "$env:TEMP\Ionize-USB-Test-Copy.txt"

Substitute the actual drive letter.
Finding: unmanaged USB storage is fully usable contrary to the SOE's intended removable-media policy.
10. UEFI / BIOS security
ATSB users could change boot order and disable Secure Boot without authentication.     ATSB SOE Penetration Test Repor…
Before reboot:
Confirm-SecureBootUEFI

And:
Get-Tpm

Also:
manage-bde -status

Then reboot into UEFI/BIOS.
Check whether you can:
enter UEFI without a password
change boot order
enable USB/external boot
disable Secure Boot
set/change firmware password
change TPM/security settings

You do not need to save destructive changes.
Take photos/screenshots of any configuration page showing it is editable without authentication.
This is potentially the same finding as ATSB if confirmed.
